Thanks for the reply. I also read the more detailed reply to /u/gorkish so I understand better your architecture and the reasons for your design choices. If you to manage to reallise a receiver with &gt;20ENOB, and have some tight filtering at the i/f stages, you will for sure have a world-class receiver on your hands.

&amp;#x200B;

Do you have any plans to implement [ORGANIZATION] or some other common protocol? I feel this would greatly increase the desirability of this radio due to the decent existing s/w. I use [ORGANIZATION] and am pretty familiar with it, and I would not relish using it as a primary control for a ham rig.

Just to clarify... this is not my project. This is all [PERSON] (the developer of PiHPSDR) work. PiHPSDR is a control surface for the [ORGANIZATION] protocol, which is used by the [ORGANIZATION] series of transceivers, the [PRODUCT] SDRs and many others. The HL2 group (myself included) contributed to [PERSON]'s project,including donating a HL2 for him to make the necessary changes to ensure compatibility, so yes, your HL2v5 would work fine with it. If you don't want to source all the bits yourself, there are other options with varying degrees of 'turn-key-ness'. [PRODUCT] does a full PiHPSDR, and a kit-of-parts lacking just the [ORGANIZATION] and the display. They are expensive though... the entire BOM for complete [ORGANIZATION] including front panel and enclosure is [MONEY], but requires some work (soldering, assembly). The Kit from [PRODUCT] labs is [MONEY], but requires a Pi4 and display which is a further $[MONEY]. The fully assembled version from [ORGANIZATION] is $[MONEY].

